.TH MSIP-DRIVE 1 2026-10-19 "Peios installer 0.1.13"
.SH NAME
msip-drive \- drive an MSIP conversation non-interactively
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B msip-drive
.RB [ \-\-socket
.IR PATH ]
.RB [ \-\-kind
.IR NAME ]
.RB [ \-\-set
.IR REF=VALUE ] ...
.RB [ \-\-set-file
.IR REF=PATH ] ...
.RB [ \-\-press
.IR REF ] ...
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B msip-drive
is a scriptable Message Surface Interaction Protocol (MSIP) client.
It prints turns, supplies values to named inputs, and consumes action
references in command-line order.
It understands the protocol rather than one installer flow.
.PP
Starting the client can attach to the daemon's existing conversation.
The client has no conversation-identity guard and does not require a fresh
conversation before sending queued actions.
Use an exclusive, private daemon socket when a fresh operation is required.
.PP
Actions are consumed on new turns, not updates to the same turn.
A rejected input or rescan can leave the client waiting even with actions
queued.
There is no built-in deadline.
Stopping the client does not cancel an active daemon job.
.PP
Secret elements are masked in the trace.
The trace prints only a tail of stored log lines and is not an exhaustive
job journal.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-\-socket " PATH"
Connect to PATH instead of
.IR /run/installerd.sock .
.TP
.BI \-\-kind " NAME"
Start NAME instead of the
.B install
conversation.
.TP
.BI \-\-set " REF=VALUE"
Provide VALUE when the named input is available.
Repeat this option to supply more than one value.
VALUE is visible in the process argument list and must not contain a secret.
.TP
.BI \-\-set-file " REF=PATH"
Provide the exact UTF-8 contents of PATH when the named enabled input appears.
The value is read without placing it in the process argument list.
Repeat this option to supply more than one file.
.TP
.BI \-\-press " REF"
Invoke REF on the next actionable turn.
Repeat this option to queue more actions; order is significant.
.TP
.BR \-h , " \-\-help"
Show command help.
.TP
.BR \-V , " \-\-version"
Show the program version.
.SH SECURITY
Use a protected socket and input files appropriate to the operation.
Values supplied with
.B \-\-set
are visible in the process argument list.
Use
.B \-\-set-file
for secrets; it reads the exact UTF-8 file contents without trimming a final
newline.
Protect the file itself and the captured output.
Only inputs marked secret by the daemon are masked in the trace.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
The conversation ended with a complete result, or help or version was shown.
An accepted restart request does not establish that a disk boot succeeded.
.TP
.B 1
Connection, protocol, input-file, or flow failure, or no action remained
for an actionable turn.
.TP
.B 2
Invalid command-line arguments.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR install-tui (1),
.BR installerd (8),
.BR oobed (8)
